-- THE CTOR-EXPORT INDEX MUST NOT OFFER A MODULE-PRIVATE CONSTRUCTOR.
--
-- `backend/private_mangle.ctorExportEntries` answers "may an IMPORTER name this
-- constructor?".  It used to answer from an UNGATED table — every `DData` the unit
-- declares, private ones included — and its own comment defended that as harmless:
-- *"the import side only resolves what the importing unit actually brings into
-- scope, and unmangled-because-private is harmless."*  That is false whenever a
-- module exports a TYPE and privately declares a CONSTRUCTOR of the same spelling,
-- which is exactly what pvmod does.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE any capture and independently of any
-- engine: `Zt` in this file can only be pubmod's public arity-1 constructor —
-- pvmod's `Zt(..)` member names pvmod's TYPE `Zt`, whose only constructor is `Mk`,
-- and pvmod's arity-2 `Zt` belongs to the private `Hidden` and is exported by
-- nothing.  So `unQ (Zt 7)` = `7` and `unP (Mk 5)` = `5`: `7` then `5`.
-- The typechecker agrees independently — a probe binding `w = Zt 7` in this module
-- reports `w : TQ`, pubmod's type.
--
-- ⚠️ COUNTERFACTUAL — THE COUNTERFACTUAL IS NOT THE MERGE BASE.  This program is
-- CORRECT on bfee53b4 and was broken by an INTERMEDIATE commit on this same branch
-- (6f63c710, the #1300 fix), which taught the wild branch to ask the bare-ctor
-- question that the ungated table then answered wrongly.  Measured with only
-- `compiler/backend/private_mangle.mdk` restored to 6f63c710 and the binary rebuilt:
--     $ medaka run entry.mdk        → 7 / 5, exit 0
--     $ medaka build entry.mdk -o e → exit 0
--     $ ./e                         → runtime error [E-NONEXHAUSTIVE-MATCH], exit 1
-- and on bfee53b4 the same program prints `7` / `5` at exit 0 on both engines.  So
-- this cell grades a REGRESSION INTERNAL TO THE BRANCH, and a diff against `main`
-- would not have shown it.
--
-- 🚨 THE IMPORT ORDER IS THE TRIGGER AND MUST NOT BE "TIDIED": pvmod must stay
-- FIRST.  The rename map is first-entry-wins over decl order
-- (`mangleUnitU`'s `omFromPairs (reverseL rmList)`), so with pvmod second its bogus
-- entry loses the race and the same broken compiler produces a CORRECT binary.  The
-- permuted twin `ctor_scope_private_ctor_not_offered_permuted/` pins that order and
-- was measured GREEN on the very same broken build — which is the point: a value
-- golden cannot catch an over-widening, only the permutation pair can, and either
-- cell alone would have read as proof the compiler was fine.
import pvmod.{Zt(..), unP}
import pubmod.{Zt, unQ}

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (intToString (unQ (Zt 7)))
  putStrLn (intToString (unP (Mk 5)))
